%n [š]akin | kibsu(m) | šutēšur | padānu(m)-ma | ... kibs[u(m)]
#note: @bib{RN117@258}: “As van Dijk also noted, [these lines are] similar to a couplet of the late version: @i{ašar Ḫumbāba ittallaku šakin kibsu} / @i{ḫarrānātu šutēšurāma ṭubbat girru} (SB V 4–5). Here it is evidently direct speech, not narrative. At the end of l. 15 there may be dittography: @akk{ša#-ki!#-in!# ki#-ib#-s[u]}? The spelling @i{šu-te-ši-ir} for the regular OB stative @i{šutēšur} finds a parallel in another literary tablet from Tell Harmal, the fable of Tamarisk and Date-Palm, in which the construct state of the infinitive is likewise rendered (@bib{RN432@155, IM 53946, 4}: @akk{[a-n]a šu-te-ši-ir ma-tim}). An orthography attested twice over is more easily seen as a reflection of local pronunciation or dialect than as a spelling mistake. As Lambert points out (@bib{RN432@328}), the regular infinitive @i{šu-te-šu-ur} also occurs at Tell Harmal, in a bilingual excerpt of @i{Inninšagurra} (now @i{TIM} IX 21, 9, ed. @bib{sjoeberg1975in-nin@188, 118}).”
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 4
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 5

%n ulidka | littum || ša supūri | ninsumuna
#note: @bib{RN117@259}: “Or ‘folds’. These words parallel those with which Ḫumbaba acknowledges Gilgameš’s divine origin at this point in the later text (SB V <168>): @i{u ilitti ša} [@i{Rīmat-Ninsun}] @i{atta}, and echo more closely still Enkidu’s acceptance of Gilgameš’s royal status at the end of the Pennsylvania tablet: @i{kīma ištēnma ummaka ūlidka} / @i{rīmtum ša supūrim} (or @i{supūrī}) @i{Ninsunna} (OB II 234–7). For the long form of the goddess’s name see Chapter 4, the section on Ninsun.”
// cf. L I.4 OB "II" 234-235
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 168

%n luṣṣurkku(m) | erēnam | šurmēnam | supālam | šīhūt[im | iṣṣ]ī
#note: The new reading @i{luṣṣurkku}(@i{m}) seems compatible with the copy [ARG]. See @bib{RN117@259}: “(…). The three trees mentioned here occur together in Sumerian literature as sources of timber for grand building-projects: @sux{{giš}eren {giš}šu.ur₂.min₃ {giš}za.ba.lum {giš}taškarin} (Curse of Akkade 134); @sux{{giš}eren {giš}šu.ur₂.me {giš}za.ba.lum} (Gudea Cyl. A xii 5). In Akkadian note Mt Ḫašur as @i{šad} @akk{{giš}}@i{erēni}(eren) @i{bi-šit} @akk{{giš}}@i{erēni}(eren) @akk{{giš}}@i{šurmēni}(šur.mìn) @akk{{giš}}@i{ši-qit-ti} @akk{{giš}}@i{su-pa-li}, ‘mountain of cedar, place of origin(?) of cedar, cypress, almond, @i{supālu}-juniper, etc.’ (@i{BBR} 75 obv. 6–7; SB divination prayer). The phrase @i{šīḫūtim iṣṣī simātu ēkallim} is reminiscent of the passage of the late text in which Ḫumbaba, pleading for his life, promises to reserve various kinds of timber for his conqueror (SB V 154–5): @i{luṣṣurka asa} […] / @i{iṣṣī bālti ēkal}[@i{li} …]. The reversing of the usual order of noun and adjective probably stresses the latter: see Chapter 9, the section on Some features of language and style.”
// cf. L I.4 MB "Ug₂" b+12
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 176

%n [s]imatu (|) ēkalli | waldam (|) ṣērim || mitlukam | ileʾʾi
#note: @bib{RN117@259}: “The last four words recur in OB IM 19 and can be identified as a stock line. The phrase @i{waldam ṣērim} can be added to the list of phrases that use this construction, as studied by @bib{reiner1984damqam@175–82 (Type 3)}. This line is an older version of the line and a half which conclude at least two dream episodes in SB IV (26–7: [@i{š}]@i{a iʾʾaldamma ina ṣ}[@i{ēri mitluka ileʾʾi}] / [@i{iza}]@i{kkara ana ibrīšu}, 107: [@i{taʾʾald}]@i{amma ina ṣēri mitluka nileʾʾ}[@i{i}]). Another variant occurs in OB Harmal₂ 18–19.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "IV" 26
// cf. L I.4 SB "IV" 107
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 177
// cf. L I.4 OB "IM" 19
